From dacfd4c878a12409656ccd7552e3547ab5d8222e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: teohondascully <121736842+teohondascully@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 16:27:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(eval): gate 6 becomes a program instead of a sentence

The agent-journey evaluation protocol says an actor whose decision policy can reach FactorySim, target
cells, inventories, resource lists, objective IDs or world-event state makes the run INVALID rather than
low-scoring. Until now that was prose, and prose cannot be enforced against a script.

TWO ORTHOGONAL RULES, BECAUSE EITHER ALONE IS THEATRE.

Every class_name in this project is a global reachable by bare name from any script, so an actor handed
the strictest feed imaginable can write FactorySim.new() on its next line and never touch it. Rule 1 reads
the actor's source and refuses 49 project globals plus 20 engine escapes. The 49 are DERIVED from the tree
on every run: a hand-written list of forbidden names is correct the day it is written and silently wrong
after the next class lands, which is the failure this repository keeps finding in its own instruments.

Rule 2 exists because an actor can name nothing forbidden and still be HANDED a live sim, a parameter
arriving already constructed with its type chosen by the caller. The entry point takes exactly one
argument and it is the feed.

    clean_actor.gd      VALID
    leaky_actor.gd      INVALID, 1 name: FactorySim
    smuggled_actor.gd   INVALID by ARGUMENT COUNT, having named nothing
    play_agent.gd       INVALID, 6 names and 2 arguments

The last line is the readiness report's finding reproduced by a program rather than by reading, and it is
not a defect in play_agent.gd. That actor is the deterministic driver and reading the sim is why it is
cheap enough for a sweep; the protocol's reachability oracle is privileged for the same good reason. The
judged actor is a different actor, which is the whole point.

CONTROLS THAT TRAVEL INSIDE THE CHECK, run every time rather than once elsewhere, because a boundary
checker whose ability to say no was established somewhere else can rot into a rubber stamp between runs.

Both controls were wrong first, and the record is worth more than the fix. A fresh worktree has no
.godot, so every class_name fails to resolve, the smuggler fixture would not compile, and "did not parse"
was printed as "refused by its signature" -- a real rejection that meant nothing. And a script that fails
to parse still loads as an object with an empty method list, which the first version reported as "no
entry point", which would have sent an author to add one they already had. The rules return a REASON now
and the controls assert which rule fired, not merely that something did.

The evaluation remains INVALID: one gate of six. Two are unmeasured and one is satisfied over a smaller
population than it names. Nothing here is registered in the harness, by instruction.
