@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+# Lane A — agent-journey readiness prototype
+
+    worktree     /tmp/sinkforge-agent-journey-eval   (detached, created from main at 4b7e160)
+    owner        the canonical session; one owner, no second writer
+    expiry       delete once its patch and receipts are archived, or on the director's next review,
+                 whichever comes first. It is a laboratory and not a product line.
+    merge gate   integration only when gate 6 is mechanically enforced, gates 2 to 5 have evidence, and
+                 the first manual pilot produces a valid receipt. Cherry-pick reviewed commits; never
+                 merge this worktree wholesale.
+
+## What this lane may touch
+
+The player-visible feed and adapter, actor calibration, loop detection, scenario and receipt schemas,
+readiness checks, journey orchestration, evaluation documentation, and tests for those. **Not** `scenes/`,
+`src/`, gameplay behaviour, Freight or sink design, HUD or terrain presentation, harness thresholds, the
+canonical harness registry, canonical captures, or user saves.
+
+**Nothing here is registered in `tools/run_harness.sh`.** The director's instruction is that this lane must
+not become a per-commit green, and a readiness check that runs on every commit stops being read.
+
+## Gate 6 — DONE, and it is the only gate this lane has closed
+
+`docs/AGENT_PLAY_EVALUATION_PROTOCOL.md` gate 6 says an actor whose decision policy can reach `FactorySim`,
+target cells, inventories, resource lists, objective IDs or world-event state makes the evaluation INVALID
+rather than low-scoring. That was a sentence in a document. It is now a program:
+
+    bash tools/with_machine.sh --headless --script res://tools/eval/check_actor_boundary.gd -- <actor.gd>
+
+    tools/eval/player_feed.gd              pixels, screen size, audio flag, the verb vocabulary, screen-space
+                                           pointing, and an input log. Nine public methods, no world state.
+    tools/eval/check_actor_boundary.gd     two orthogonal rules and four controls
+    tools/eval/fixtures/clean_actor.gd     negative control: pixels only
+    tools/eval/fixtures/leaky_actor.gd     positive control for the name rule
+    tools/eval/fixtures/smuggled_actor.gd  positive control for the signature rule
+
+**Rule 1, the names.** Every `class_name` in this project is a global reachable by bare name from any
+script, so a perfect feed prevents nothing on its own. The denylist is 49 names DERIVED from the source
+tree each run plus 20 enumerated engine escapes, because a hand-written list is correct on the day it is
+written and quietly wrong afterwards.
+
+**Rule 2, the signature.** An actor that names nothing forbidden can still be HANDED a live sim, since a
+parameter arrives already constructed and its type is the caller's choice. The entry point takes exactly
+one argument and it is the feed.
+
+**The verdict against the two actors that matter:**
+
+    tools/eval/fixtures/clean_actor.gd   VALID
+    tools/play_agent.gd                  INVALID: reaches MachineDef, MachineState, FactorySim, PlayAgent,
+                                         Player, MainView, and takes two entry arguments
+
+That second line is the readiness report's finding reproduced by a program rather than by reading, and it
+is also the reason `play_agent.gd` must keep its privileges: it is the deterministic driver, not the judged
+actor. The protocol's reachability oracle is privileged for the same good reason.
+
+## Two things this lane got wrong first, recorded because they will recur
+
+**A fresh worktree has no `.godot`, so every `class_name` fails to resolve and every fixture fails to
+parse.** The smuggler control was refused on its first run and reported as refused BY ITS SIGNATURE. It was
+refused because it did not compile. Import the worktree before believing any result from it.
+
+**A script that fails to parse still loads as an object with an empty method list**, which the first version
+read as "no entry point". The rejection was right and its reason was invented. Both were fixed by making
+the rule return a REASON and having the control assert which rule fired, rather than that something did.
+
+## Gates still open, and none of them is closed by the above
+
+    1 safe isolation      likely satisfied; wants an explicit receipt rather than an inference
+    2 truthful route      UNMEASURED. Nothing asserts the opening route is completable through
+                          player-facing verbs with no injected inventory
+    3 unmanufactured      UNMEASURED. Has to be established by playing it, not by grep
+    4 legible route       partially satisfied over a SMALLER population than the gate names
+    5 evidence feed       likely satisfied; wants an explicit receipt
+    6 actor boundary      DONE, above
+
+**The evaluation stays INVALID.** One gate closed out of six is not readiness, and the report that said
+"one gate short" was wrong in the other direction: it is one failed, two unmeasured, one narrower than its
+gate. No pilot may be run or scored until all six hold.
diff --git a/tools/eval/check_actor_boundary.gd b/tools/eval/check_actor_boundary.gd
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5c4f555
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/eval/check_actor_boundary.gd
@@ -0,0 +1,303 @@
+extends SceneTree
+
+## GATE 6, MADE MECHANICAL. `docs/AGENT_PLAY_EVALUATION_PROTOCOL.md` says an actor whose decision policy
+## can reach `FactorySim`, target cells, inventories, resource lists, objective IDs or world-event state
+## makes the whole evaluation INVALID rather than low-scoring. That is a claim about a program, so a
+## program can settle it, and until something does the gate is a sentence in a document that an actor
+## cannot read.
+##
+## THE THREAT IS NOT A LEAKY FEED, WHICH IS WHY A NARROW FEED PROVES NOTHING ON ITS OWN. Every
+## `class_name` in this project is a GLOBAL, reachable by bare name from any script. An actor handed the
+## strictest feed imaginable can still write `FactorySim.new()` on its next line and never touch it.
+## `tools/eval/fixtures/leaky_actor.gd` does exactly that, in legal GDScript, and it is this file's
+## positive control: if it passes, nothing here is enforcing anything.
+##
+## THE DENYLIST IS DERIVED, NOT WRITTEN DOWN. It is read out of the repository every run, so a class added
+## tomorrow is covered tonight without anyone remembering this file exists. A hand-maintained list of
+## forbidden names would be correct on the day it was written and quietly wrong afterwards, which is the
+## failure this project keeps finding in its own instruments.
+##
+##     bash tools/with_machine.sh --headless --script res://tools/eval/check_actor_boundary.gd -- <actor.gd>
+##
+## Exit 0 the actor is inside the boundary; 1 it is outside; 2 the check could not be performed, which is
+## NOT a pass. Not registered in the harness: the director's instruction is that this lane must not become
+## a per-commit green.
+
+## Reaches outside a script that no denylist of project names would catch. These are engine surfaces, so
+## unlike the project globals they cannot be derived and have to be enumerated; each one is a way to get
+## from a RefCounted with no references to the live scene, the filesystem, or the input layer underneath
+## the feed.
+const ESCAPES: Array[String] = [
+	"get_tree", "get_node", "find_child", "get_parent", "owner",
+	"Engine.", "ClassDB", "ResourceLoader", "ProjectSettings", "instance_from_id",
+	"load(", "preload(", "FileAccess", "DirAccess", "OS.",
+	"RenderingServer", "PhysicsServer2D", "AudioServer", "DisplayServer", "Performance",
+]
+## The feed's own class, and the actor base, are the two names an actor is SUPPOSED to say.
+const ALLOWED_GLOBALS: Array[String] = ["EvalPlayerFeed"]
+## A member on the feed itself whose name reads like privileged state. This catches the feed growing a
+## leak later, which is the direction nobody watches: the denylist above guards the actor, and nothing
+## would guard the object the actor is handed.
+const LEAKY_WORDS: Array[String] = [
+	"sim", "cell", "inventory", "pack", "ore", "lode", "objective", "machine", "world",
+	"coord", "tile", "solid", "resource", "recipe", "target", "route",
+]
+
+
+func _initialize() -> void:
+	var args: PackedStringArray = OS.get_cmdline_user_args()
+	if args.is_empty():
+		printerr("check_actor_boundary: name the actor script to judge")
+		quit(2)
+		return
+	var globals: Array[String] = _project_globals()
+	if globals.size() < 20:
+		printerr("check_actor_boundary: only %d project globals found; the denylist did not build, and an "
+			% globals.size() + "empty denylist passes everything")
+		quit(2)
+		return
+	print("== gate 6: the judged actor may not reach past its feed ==")
+	print("denylist: %d project globals derived from source, %d engine escapes enumerated"
+		% [globals.size(), ESCAPES.size()])
+
+	var fails: int = 0
+	fails += _judge_feed_surface()
+	# THE CONTROLS RUN EVERY TIME AND INSIDE THE SAME PROCESS, not as a separate invocation somebody has to
+	# remember. A boundary checker whose ability to say no is established once, elsewhere, is a boundary
+	# checker that can rot into a rubber stamp between runs.
+	var leaky: Dictionary = _scan("res://tools/eval/fixtures/leaky_actor.gd", globals)
+	var clean: Dictionary = _scan("res://tools/eval/fixtures/clean_actor.gd", globals)
+	# The smuggler names nothing forbidden and must still be refused, by the signature rule rather than by
+	# the denylist. Its control belongs beside the others: a rule whose only exercise is the file it was
+	# written for is a rule nobody will notice going quiet.
+	var smug: Dictionary = _judge_entry("res://tools/eval/fixtures/smuggled_actor.gd")
+	var smug_named: int = int(_scan("res://tools/eval/fixtures/smuggled_actor.gd", globals)["hits"])
+	if String(smug["why"]) == "argc" and smug_named == 0:
+		print("  PASS: CONTROL the smuggler is REJECTED by ARGUMENT COUNT (%s), having named nothing"
+			% smug["detail"])
+	elif bool(smug["ok"]):
+		print("  FAIL: CONTROL the smuggler was ACCEPTED; a second parameter is an unwatched door")
+		fails += 1
+	else:
+		print("  FAIL: CONTROL the smuggler was refused for '%s' (%s), not by the signature rule. %s"
+			% [smug["why"], smug["detail"],
+			"The denylist fired too, so this proves nothing about signatures."
+				if smug_named > 0 else "A refusal for the wrong reason is not enforcement."])
+		fails += 1
+	var clean_entry: Dictionary = _judge_entry("res://tools/eval/fixtures/clean_actor.gd")
+	if bool(clean_entry["ok"]):
+		print("  PASS: CONTROL the pixels-only actor's signature is ACCEPTED (one arg, typed %s)"
+			% clean_entry["detail"])
+	else:
+		print("  FAIL: CONTROL the pixels-only actor's signature was refused for '%s': %s"
+			% [clean_entry["why"], clean_entry["detail"]])
+		fails += 1
+	if int(leaky.get("hits", 0)) > 0:
+		print("  PASS: CONTROL the deliberately leaky actor is REJECTED (%d reach%s: %s)"
+			% [leaky["hits"], "" if int(leaky["hits"]) == 1 else "es", ", ".join(leaky["names"])])
+	else:
+		print("  FAIL: CONTROL the deliberately leaky actor PASSED, so this check cannot say no")
+		fails += 1
+	if int(clean.get("hits", 0)) == 0:
+		print("  PASS: CONTROL the pixels-only actor is ACCEPTED, so the rule is not simply refusing")
+	else:
+		print("  FAIL: CONTROL the pixels-only actor was rejected for %s" % [clean["names"]])
+		fails += 1
+
+	for a: String in args:
+		var path: String = a if a.begins_with("res://") else "res://" + a
+		var r: Dictionary = _scan(path, globals)
+		if int(r.get("error", 0)) != 0:
+			print("  FAIL: %s could not be read, which is not a pass" % path)
+			fails += 1
+			continue
+		if int(r["hits"]) == 0:
+			print("  PASS: %s reaches nothing outside its feed (%d identifiers screened)"
+				% [path, r["screened"]])
+		else:
+			print("  FAIL: %s reaches %d privileged name(s): %s"
+				% [path, r["hits"], ", ".join(r["names"])])
+			fails += 1
+		var ent: Dictionary = _judge_entry(path)
+		if bool(ent["ok"]):
+			print("  PASS: %s is handed exactly one argument and it is typed %s" % [path, ent["detail"]])
+		else:
+			print("  FAIL: %s entry signature refused (%s): %s" % [path, ent["why"], ent["detail"]])
+			fails += 1
+
+	if fails == 0:
+		print("check_actor_boundary: VALID -- the judged actor is inside the boundary")
+		quit(0)
+	else:
+		print("check_actor_boundary: INVALID -- %d failure(s); the protocol says INVALID, not low-scoring"
+			% fails)
+		quit(1)
+
+
+## Every `class_name` declared anywhere in the project. Read from source rather than from `ClassDB`,
+## because the point is the set of names an actor's script can WRITE, and that set is a property of the
+## source tree.
+func _project_globals() -> Array[String]:
+	var out: Array[String] = []
+	var stack: Array[String] = ["res://"]
+	while not stack.is_empty():
+		var dir_path: String = stack.pop_back()
+		var d: DirAccess = DirAccess.open(dir_path)
+		if d == null:
+			continue
+		d.list_dir_begin()
+		var n: String = d.get_next()
+		while n != "":
+			if n.begins_with("."):
+				n = d.get_next()
+				continue
+			var full: String = dir_path.path_join(n)
+			if d.current_is_dir():
+				stack.append(full)
+			elif n.ends_with(".gd"):
+				for line: String in FileAccess.get_file_as_string(full).split("\n"):
+					if line.begins_with("class_name "):
+						var nm: String = line.substr(11).strip_edges().split(" ")[0]
+						if nm != "" and not ALLOWED_GLOBALS.has(nm) and not out.has(nm):
+							out.append(nm)
+			n = d.get_next()
+		d.list_dir_end()
+	return out
+
+
+## Read one actor and report which privileged names it reaches. Comments and string literals are removed
+## first: a file that DISCUSSES `FactorySim` in a docstring is not a file that reaches it, and a checker
+## that cannot tell those apart teaches people to stop writing docstrings.
+func _scan(path: String, globals: Array[String]) -> Dictionary:
+	if not FileAccess.file_exists(path):
+		return {"error": 1, "hits": 0, "names": [], "screened": 0}
+	var src: String = _strip(FileAccess.get_file_as_string(path))
+	var names: Array[String] = []
+	for g: String in globals:
+		if _has_identifier(src, g):
+			names.append(g)
+	for e: String in ESCAPES:
+		if src.contains(e) and not names.has(e):
+			names.append(e)
+	return {"error": 0, "hits": names.size(), "names": names, "screened": globals.size() + ESCAPES.size()}
+
+
+## An identifier match, not a substring one. `Score` must not fire on `_scored`, and `Art` must not fire
+## on `_start`; a substring rule over 49 short names would reject every actor ever written and the
+## rejection would look exactly like enforcement.
+func _has_identifier(src: String, name: String) -> bool:
+	var from: int = 0
+	while true:
+		var i: int = src.find(name, from)
+		if i < 0:
+			return false
+		var before: String = "" if i == 0 else src[i - 1]
+		var after_i: int = i + name.length()
+		var after: String = "" if after_i >= src.length() else src[after_i]
+		if not _wordish(before) and not _wordish(after):
+			return true
+		from = i + 1
+	return false
+
+
+func _wordish(c: String) -> bool:
+	if c == "":
+		return false
+	return c == "_" or (c >= "0" and c <= "9") or (c.to_lower() >= "a" and c.to_lower() <= "z")
+
+
+## Comments and string literals out, so the scan reads code and not prose.
+func _strip(src: String) -> String:
+	var out: String = ""
+	var in_str: bool = false
+	var quote: String = ""
+	var in_com: bool = false
+	for i: int in src.length():
+		var c: String = src[i]
+		if in_com:
+			if c == "\n":
+				in_com = false
+				out += c
+			continue
+		if in_str:
+			if c == quote and (i == 0 or src[i - 1] != "\\"):
+				in_str = false
+			continue
+		if c == "#":
+			in_com = true
+			continue
+		if c == "\"" or c == "'":
+			in_str = true
+			quote = c
+			continue
+		out += c
+	return out
+
+
+## The other direction: the feed itself must not grow a privileged accessor. Nothing else watches this,
+## because the denylist above reads the ACTOR and a leak added here would be invisible to it.
+func _judge_feed_surface() -> int:
+	var script: GDScript = load("res://tools/eval/player_feed.gd") as GDScript
+	if script == null:
+		print("  FAIL: the feed script did not load, so its surface was never judged")
+		return 1
+	var bad: Array[String] = []
+	var seen: int = 0
+	for m: Dictionary in script.get_script_method_list():
+		var nm: String = String(m.get("name", ""))
+		if nm.begins_with("_") and nm != "_init":
+			continue
+		seen += 1
+		for w: String in LEAKY_WORDS:
+			if nm.to_lower().contains(w) and not bad.has(nm):
+				bad.append(nm)
+	if seen < 5:
+		print("  FAIL: only %d public method(s) found on the feed; the surface was not read" % seen)
+		return 1
+	if bad.is_empty():
+		print("  PASS: the feed's %d public methods name nothing privileged" % seen)
+		return 0
+	print("  FAIL: the feed exposes %s" % [bad])
+	return 1
+
+
+## THE DOOR THE DENYLIST CANNOT SEE. An actor that names nothing forbidden can still be HANDED a live sim,
+## because a parameter arrives already constructed and its type is the orchestrator's choice rather than
+## the actor's. So the entry point takes exactly one argument and it is the feed. Anything else is refused
+## without reading another line, which also means the rule holds for an actor written by someone who never
+## read this file.
+##
+## IT RETURNS A REASON AND NOT A VERDICT, because the first version returned a bare failure and its own
+## control passed for the wrong reason: in a freshly created worktree there is no `.godot`, so every
+## `class_name` fails to resolve, the smuggler fixture would not parse, and "did not load" was reported as
+## "refused by its signature". The rejection was real and meant nothing. A control that cannot tell which
+## rule fired is not a control, so the caller now asserts the reason.
+func _judge_entry(path: String) -> Dictionary:
+	var script: GDScript = load(path) as GDScript
+	if script == null:
+		return {"ok": false, "why": "noload", "detail": "did not load at all"}
+	# A SCRIPT THAT FAILED TO PARSE STILL COMES BACK AS AN OBJECT, with an empty method list, and the first
+	# version read that as "no _init". The rejection was correct and its reason was invented: an author
+	# with a type error would have been told to add an entry point they already had. Ask whether it parsed
+	# before asking what it declares.
+	if not script.can_instantiate():
+		return {"ok": false, "why": "noparse",
+			"detail": "loaded but did not parse, so nothing about its signature could be read"}
+	var init: Dictionary = {}
+	for m: Dictionary in script.get_script_method_list():
+		if String(m.get("name", "")) == "_init":
+			init = m
+			break
+	if init.is_empty():
+		return {"ok": false, "why": "noinit", "detail": "no _init, so nothing states what it is handed"}
+	var args: Array = init.get("args", [])
+	if args.size() != 1:
+		return {"ok": false, "why": "argc",
+			"detail": "takes %d entry arguments; exactly one is allowed and it is the feed" % args.size()}
+	var a: Dictionary = args[0]
+	var cls: String = String(a.get("class_name", ""))
+	if int(a.get("type", 0)) != TYPE_OBJECT or cls == "":
+		return {"ok": false, "why": "untyped",
+			"detail": "entry argument is type %d / class %s, so it accepts anything"
+				% [int(a.get("type", 0)), "<none>" if cls == "" else cls]}
+	return {"ok": true, "why": "typed", "detail": cls}
